          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score the most points individually over a Rubber. Gains are tracked per player, not per team.',
          gameplay: "Four players receive 13 cards each. An auction determines the contract (trump or no-trump) and forms a temporary partnership: the highest bidder is the Declarer and the player who accepts the bid becomes the Acceptor; the other two defend. The Acceptor's hand is exposed as dummy after the opening lead. Players then play 13 tricks: must follow suit if possible; highest card of the led suit wins the trick, or a trump wins if unable to follow. Points are scored individually (above and below the line); the side that wins two games wins the rubber.",
          keyRules: [
            'Deal 13 cards each; no fixed partners.',
            'Auction establishes contract and temporary Declarer-Acceptor partnership.',
            'Must follow suit when possible; highest of led suit or trump wins the trick.',
            'Individual scoring over a rubber; first to two games wins the rubber.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Choose your partner wisely! Only accept a bid if you have the cards to support that suit. Since you are playing for your own score, don't help a partner who is significantly ahead of you in points unless you have to. If you are the Bidder, you want an Acceptor who holds the 'High Honors' (A-K-Q) in your suit.",
          intermediate: "The 'Partner Sabotage' (Defensive): If you are defending, you are not just trying to stop the contractâ€”you are trying to ensure the declarer (who is your rival in the overall game) doesn't gain points 'Below the Line'. Defending a 1-level contract is worth more to you psychologically than winning a 4-level contract yourself if it puts you in the lead.",
          advanced: "Market Bidding: In Pirate Bridge, bidding is like a negotiation. If you bid 'One Heart' and no one accepts, your hand is 'Valueless'. You must learn to 'Invite' acceptance. Sometimes, bidding a slightly weaker suit that you know another player has 'Stop' in is better than bidding your longest suit that no one can help with. You need a partner to win.",
          tips: [
            "The Acceptor scores ONLY 'Above the Line'. This means being an Acceptor won't help you win a Game directly, but it builds your total score for the Rubber.",
            "Don't accept a bid by your closest rival if you can avoid it!",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
